  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 158 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nDespite a cold front moving through on Wednesday, Thursday's \ntemperatures will remain near to slightly above normal for this \ntime of year with temperatures in the 50s across western and \nnorth central Nebraska. An upper level ridge builds in late \nThursday keeping overnight lows fairly mild, in fact \nunseasonably warm with locations across the southwest Nebraska \nup to north central Nebraska remaining in the upper 30s to low \n40s Thursday night. Friday highs will warm into the 60s across \nsouthwest and north central Nebraska and the majority of the \nSandhills. The exception will be the northwest Sandhills where \nthe influence of the next system moving into western Nebraska \nFriday will keep temperatures cooler. Ahead of the system 850 \ntemps around 11 to 13 degrees celsius will mix to the sfc with \ntemperatures reaching the mid to upper 60s Friday afternoon. \n\nAs for the incoming system Friday into Saturday, guidance continues \nto favor a more open wave solution, and has the system coming on \nshore with an open wave in the last several runs over the last \ncouple days, leading to greater confidence in a weaker weekend \nsystem. That being said there will still be the potential to see \nprecipitation with this upcoming system as moisture does advect back \ninto the Central Conus ahead of the system on Friday, although \nlimited moisture. Confidence still remains low in the exact \ntrack of the system, which will determine where the greatest \npotential for precipitation occurs. Have left precipitation \nchances in, keeping below a 50 percent chance. At this time have\nlower confidence in thunderstorm chances for the forecast area \nas instability is weak, thus expect precipitation to be in the \nform of scattered rain showers. QPF values with this system are \nexpected to remain light with probs of less than 40 percent for \nqpf values greater than 0.05 of an inch. \n\nDepending on how this system evolves and tracks, cooler air may \ninfiltrate into the High Plains for the weekend and could see \ntemperatures below normal in the 40s, but again this could change \ngiven the track of the upcoming system and will see a better \nevolution once it moves onshore.",
